  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nLow pressure is moving across northern Ontario this morning with a \ncold front trailing into northern MN. Radar returns show some weak \nechoes across northern MN but with the dry air in the low levels we \ndon't anticipate much of this activity to make it to the \nsurface, at best expecting a few sprinkles. The frontal boundary\nwill march slowly across the Northland today with winds turning\nfrom southerly to northerly with its passage. High pressure \nwill build in behind the boundary with cloud cover largely \nclearing out by mid day. This will allow ample time for \ntemperatures to recover with another warm day expected as \nafternoon high temperatures climb into the 60s and low 70s. \nOvernight, radiational cooling paired with lightly mixed winds \nand low level moisture could lead to areas of fog developing. \nHREF and REFS suggest a 30-50% probability of fog occurrence \nacross the Northland. \n\nTomorrow:\n\nTransient surface high pressure will drift eastward across the \nregion Thursday as an upper-level ridge builds over the northern \nPlains and Canadian Prairies. Temperatures will remain well above \nnormal as winds turn southerly. Lows Thursday morning will range \nfrom the low 30s to around 40 degrees. Look for highs in the low 60s \nto low 70s, about 10-20 degrees above normal. Temps will be cooler \nfor portions of the North Shore and Arrowhead due to on-shore flow. \nThose readings will be 5-10 degrees above normal with highs in the \nmiddle 50s to low 60s.\n\nFriday through Sunday Night:\n\nThe Pacific cutoff low will get a slight nudge eastward and is \nexpected to move into the Intermountain West by Friday morning. \nSurface low pressure is forecast to develop in the Rockies and lift \nnortheastward into Saskatchewan and Manitoba by Saturday morning. A \nwarm front is forecast to lift northward across the Northland for \nthe end of the work week. Isentropic ascent in the warm sector will \nsupport a chance of rain showers Friday morning over central and \nnorth-central Minnesota and spreading eastward across the Northland \nthrough Saturday morning. The highest rain chances will be found \nnear and north of the warm front, mainly over the Iron Range and \nBorderlands Friday evening. Rain amounts will be meager, less than a \ntenth of an inch for most areas. A few locations may pick up 0.1-\n0.2\". Lingering precipitation chances will be possible Sunday night \nas upper level impulses surge northeast along the trailing boundary \nacross the Midwest. Low confidence in the Sunday precipitation as \nthis forecast package reintroduced the 20-30% chances after \nbeing absent from the last 2 packages. Temperatures Saturday \nremain around 15-25 degrees above normal as the Northland stays \nin the warm sector most of the day. Rain chance end from west to\neast Saturday and Saturday night.\n\nNext Week:\n\nSplit flow aloft will persist into early next week. The northern \nstream will transition toward northwest flow as high pressure drifts \neastward across the region. The northwesterly flow will bring cooler \nair aloft into the region. Cooler, near- normal, temperatures are \nexpected Sunday through next Tuesday. Highs will be in the mid 40s \nto low 50s. The high pressure over the region will keep precipitation\nchances low to zero.",
